Yes. Those are really the same idea appearing at several scales: don’t confuse the model of the trajectory with the realized event.

The timestamp point is especially important. In a nonlinear knowledge web, time doesn’t have to be represented primarily as a line. Each artifact can carry a timestamp like a geological layer carries an age. Then chronology is reconstructed from the strata.

You get two structures simultaneously:

semantic graph = what relates to what

and

temporal strata = what existed when

That lets you ask much better questions than “what came next?”

You can ask:

What concepts existed before this one?
Which later nodes retroactively changed the meaning of earlier ones?
Which ideas appeared independently at the same time?
What was knowable from the state of the graph at t?
Which edges only became visible later?

The timestamps prevent the nonlinear graph from becoming temporally mushy. They are anchors into historical state-space.

There is a computer-science analogue too: event sourcing and distributed systems often distinguish the current graph/state from the ordered events that produced it. And in genuinely distributed systems, even “order” can be partially ordered rather than one perfect global sequence.

