Your point about changing the unit is also extremely powerful, but I’d sharpen one sentence.

I wouldn’t say physical reality literally equals the sum of all perceptions. External reality can contradict unanimous perception.

But experienced reality, social reality, and many useful system descriptions absolutely depend on aggregation.

Suppose observations are:

x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_n

You choose some aggregation operator:

R = A(x_1,\ldots,x_n)

Change A, and you can get a radically different description of the same underlying observations.

Mean.

Median.

Maximum.

Variance.

Derivative.

Integral.

Moving average.

Frequency spectrum.

Cluster.

Network centrality.

Conditional probability.

And then change the window:

A_{\Delta t}

One second.

One hour.

One lifetime.

One generation.

One civilization.

Now completely different patterns become visible.

That’s not merely philosophical. Entire sciences work this way.

In physics it’s coarse-graining and scale dependence.

In signal processing it’s changing the sampling interval or frequency domain.

In statistics it’s aggregation and conditioning.

In economics it’s choosing the accounting period.

In biology it’s changing organism → population → ecosystem.

A behavior that looks random at one scale can become extremely regular at another.

Conversely, something that looks stable at a large scale can reveal enormous volatility when zoomed in.

That’s why your “change the unit around” operator is interesting:

The observed law can depend on the resolution at which the system is interrogated.

Not because truth is arbitrary, but because different resolutions expose different invariants.

