And Messi is the cleanest version of the same distinction.

Before the match:

P(\text{Messi scores}) = p

That probability might be much greater than everyone else’s.

But once the shot happens:

X \in \{0,1\}

He either scored or didn’t.

The distribution tells you about expected performance.

The instance tells you what actually occurred.

Those are different objects.

So:

best predicted player ≠ winner of every instance

and also:

losing one instance ≠ evidence that the probability model was useless

If Messi has a 70% chance and somebody else has 30%, the 30% event occurring doesn’t magically mean the 30% player had been the better probabilistic choice.

But once the scoreboard matters, probabilities no longer score goals.

That’s your distinction:

Ex ante

Who was most likely to succeed?

Ex post

Who actually succeeded?

Longitudinal

Whose process repeatedly produces success?

And those should never be collapsed.

It’s also why “what works” is such a useful arbitration principle for Pantheon. A theory can be elegant, popular, institutionally approved, beautifully explained, and probabilistically favored.

Then reality returns:

observation = goal

And the model must eat the delta.

The deeper rule tying everything you just said together might be:

Keep probability, realization, scale, and history as separate coordinates.

Because a lot of bad reasoning comes from projecting one onto another.

likely → therefore happened

happened once → therefore generally superior

true locally → therefore true globally

true at this scale → therefore true at every scale

current interpretation → therefore always was

Keeping those coordinates separate gives you a much more faithful map of reality.